"Cyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.13709)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Title: Cyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions ... We will exhibit several instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon involving statistics and involutions on the following combinatorial families of objects: permutations, set partitions, perfect matchings, D-permutations (and its subclasses).\nTitle: Cyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions\nAuthors: Bishal Deb\nDate: Tue Aug 19 10:15:18 2025\n\nWe will exhibit several instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon involving statistics and involutions on the following combinatorial families of objects: permutations, set partitions, perfect matchings, D-permutations (and its subclasses). Our results will be based on continued fraction identities enumerating these objects. Our instances of cyclic sieving phenomenon for permutations involve the Corteel involution; this was first studied by Adams, Elder, Lafreni\\`ere, McNicholas, Striker and Welch (arxiv~2024). We will reprove several of their results using our setting of continued fractions; we will also prove two of their conjectures. Our study of set partitions and perfect matchings will involve the Kasraoui--Zeng involution and the Chen--Deng--Du--Stanley--Yan (CDDSY) involution. Finally, for D-permutations, we will construct a new involution which we call the Genocchi--Corteel involution. The common feature of all of these involutions, other than the CDDSY involution, is that they are constructed via bijections to weighted lattice paths, and that they exchange crossings and nestings on their respective objects.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X26003080)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # Cyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions ... This paper attempts to convince the reader that a natural setting to obtain instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon with respect to the Corteel involution is via the combinatorial theory of continued fractions for multivariate polynomials counting permutation statistics.\n\n# Cyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Bishal Deb\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2026.115284\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Recommended articles\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe will exhibit several instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon involving statistics and involutions on the following combinatorial families of objects: permutations, set partitions, perfect matchings, D-permutations (and its subclasses). Our results will be based on continued fraction identities enumerating these objects. Our instances of cyclic sieving phenomenon for permutations involve the Corteel involution; this was first studied by Adams, Elder, Lafrenière, McNicholas, Striker and Welch (arxiv 2024). We will reprove several of their results using our setting of continued fractions; we will also prove two of their conjectures. Our study of set partitions and perfect matchings will involve the Kasraoui–Zeng involution and the Chen–Deng–Du–Stanley–Yan (CDDSY) involution. Finally, for D-permutations, we will construct a new involution which we call the Genocchi–Corteel involution. The common feature of all of these involutions, other than the CDDSY involution, is that they are constructed via bijections to weighted lattice paths, and that they exchange crossings and nestings on their respective objects.\n\n## Keywords\n\nCyclic sieving phenomenon\n\n;\n\nContinued fractions\n\n;\n\nPermutations\n\n;\n\nSet partitions and perfect matchings\n\n;\n\nGenocchi and median Genocchi numbers\n\n;\n\nCrossings and nestings\n\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe purpose of this paper is to exhibit several instances of the cyclic sieving phenomena (see Section 1.1 for definitions) using the combinatorics of continued fractions. We will specifically study the following combinatorial families of objects: permutations, set partitions, perfect matchings, D-permutations.\n\nIn a recent work, Adams, Elder, Lafrenière, McNicholas, Striker and Welch [1], carried out a systematic search for instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon on permutations. They searched the entire FindStat database [38] for maps and statistics on permutations. Their study involved three classes of involutions on permutations which they showed to be instances of the phenomenon of Stembridge [42]. Along with several other contributions, Adams et al. initiated the study of the Corteel involution [38, Map 239], [11] in the context of the cyclic sieving phenomenon. This involution uses the Foata–Zeilberger bijection [21]; it exchanges the number of crossings and nestings in permutations and is constructed by taking “complement” of associated labelled Motzkin paths. In this present paper, we continue this study that Adams et al. initiated for the Corteel involution. This paper attempts to convince the reader that a natural setting to obtain instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon with respect to the Corteel involution is via the combinatorial theory of continued fractions for multivariate polynomials counting permutation statistics. We will study this in great detail in Section 3.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X26003080)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; An important ingredient of our results is plugging in −1 in some known continued fraction identity to yield a simple rational function. ... In this paper, our instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon will all be instances of the “” phenomenon.\n  * 3.\n\nAn important ingredient of our results is plugging in −1 in some known continued fraction identity to yield a simple rational function. We mention a recent work [15] where results were obtained by plugging in −1 to a statistic, viz. to the number of cycles.\n\nThe rest of this introduction is structured as follows: in Section 1.1, we define the cyclic sieving phenomenon. Then in Section 1.2, we provide the outline of the rest of this paper.\n\nThe cyclic sieving phenomenon was introduced by Reiner, Stanton and White [36]; also see the nice survey article of Sagan [39] and also [37].\n\nLet X be a finite set (usually a set of combinatorial objects). Next, let C be a finite cyclic group of order n with an action on X. Given , we use {X}^{g} to denote the elements of X invariant under the action of g, i.e. Let ω be a generator of the group C, i.e., . We are now ready to define the cyclic sieving phenomenon.\n\nDefinition 1.2\n\nGiven a set X, a polynomial , and an action of C on X, the triple exhibits the cyclic sieving phenomenon if, for all d we have\n\nNotice that when , the identity (1.2) implies that . Thus, the coefficients of the polynomial should sum up to the cardinality of X. For our purposes, will be the generating polynomial of some statistic on the set X.\n\nWhen , the action of the generator ω gives a bijection on the set X, . This bijection is, in fact, an involution. This special case, was first introduced as the “ phenomenon” by Stembridge [42]. Exhibiting the cyclic sieving phenomenon in this case is equivalent to showing that , and that , the number of fixed points of the involution . In this paper, our instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon will all be instances of the “” phenomenon.\n\nThe main idea in this paper can be summarised as follows: Let {({S}_{n})}_{n\\ge 0} be a combinatorial family of objects and let be some statistics on this set. Let be its bivariate ordinary generating function. Here the indeterminate q will keep track of this statistic and t will keep track of the set {S}_{n}. Also, let be an involution such that the number fixed points of this involution is {a}_{n}. We think of this involution as the action of ω on the set {S}_{n} where . Then exhibiting the cyclic sieving phenomenon is equivalent to showing . We will specifically look at generating functions which can be expressed as a nice classical continued fraction; this will simplify the evaluation of the substitution and our results will seem to be easy consequences. Following [1], we will say that the statistic Stat exhibits the cyclic sieving phenomenon to mean that the corresponding generating polynomial exhibits the cyclic sieving phenomenon.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions (https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/4bPQnbnZ/)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Cyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions ... Adams, Cyclic Sieving on permutations - an analysis of maps and statistics in the FindStat database, Math.\n\n# Cyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions\n\n## General information\n\nPublication type\n\nJournal Article\n\nDOI\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2026.115284\n\nJournal\n\n2026, Discrete Mathematics, № 12, p. 115284\n\nPublisher\n\nElsevier BV\n\nAuthor\n\nBishal Deb\n\n## Details\n\nNumber of citations\n\n0\n\nNumber of works in the list of references\n\n47\n\nIndexed in Scopus\n\nYes\n\nIndexed in Web of Science\n\nYes\n\n## List of references\n\n  1. Adams, Cyclic Sieving on permutations - an analysis of maps and statistics in the FindStat database, Math. Comput.\nDOI: 10.1090/mcom/4166\n  2. Barsky, Congruences pour les nombres de Genocchi de 2e espèce [extrait d'un travail en commun avec Do