nt coefficients and rational generating functions, with growth constants increasing to 4$4$ $4$.\n\nSubject: Combinatorics\n\nPublish: 2026-04-24 00:52:19 UTC--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Ising model on cubic maps: arbitrary genus | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2504.00768v2)\nciteturn4search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; This series is indeed related to the Ising partition function by a change of variables. ... ## Citation ... @misc{bousquetmélou2025isingmodelcubicmaps, title={The Ising model on cubic maps: arbitrary genus}, author={Mireille Bousquet-Mélou and Ariane Carrance and Baptiste Louf}, year={2025}, eprint={2504.00768}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00768}, }\nSubmitted 12 Sept 2025\n\n# The Ising model on cubic maps: arbitrary genus\n\nMB\n\nMireille Bousquet-Mélou\n\nAC\n\nAriane Carrance\n\nBL\n\nBaptiste Louf\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe design a recursive algorithm to compute the partition function of the Ising model, summed over cubic maps with fixed size and genus. The algorithm runs in polynomial time, which is much faster than methods based on a Tutte-like, or topological, recursion. We construct this algorithm out of a partial differential equation that we derive from the first equation of the KP hierarchy satisfied by the generating function of bipartite maps. This series is indeed related to the Ising partition function by a change of variables. We also obtain inequalities on the coefficients of this partition function, which should be useful for a probabilistic study of cubic Ising maps whose genus grows linearly with their size.\n\nView Paper\n\n11 views\n\nv2\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{bousquetmélou2025isingmodelcubicmaps, title={The Ising model on cubic maps: arbitrary genus}, author={Mireille Bousquet-Mélou and Ariane Carrance and Baptiste Louf}, year={2025}, eprint={2504.00768}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00768}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMathematics of Computation (https://doi.org/10.1090/mcom/4166)\nciteturn4search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: last week; We perform a systematic study of permutation statistics and bijective maps on permutations using SageMath to search the FindStat combinatorial statistics database to identify apparent instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon (CSP). Cyclic sieving occurs on a set of objects, a statistic, and a map of order $n$ when the evaluation of the statistic generating function at the $d$th power of the primitive $n$th root of unity equals the number of fixed points under the $d$th power of the map.\n\nCyclic sieving on permutations: An analysis of maps and statistics in the FindStat database\n\nHTML articles powered by AMS MathViewer\n\nby Ashleigh Adams, Jennifer Elder, Nadia Lafrenière, Erin McNicholas, Jessica Striker and Amanda Welch\n\nMath. Comp. 96 (2027), 471-508\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1090/mcom/4166\n\nPublished electronically: December 12, 2025\n\nHTML\n\nPDF\n\nAbstract\n\nWe perform a systematic study of permutation statistics and bijective maps on permutations using SageMath to search the FindStat combinatorial statistics database to identify apparent instances of the cyclic sieving phenomenon (CSP). Cyclic sieving occurs on a set of objects, a statistic, and a map of order $n$ when the evaluation of the statistic generating function at the $d$th power of the primitive $n$th root of unity equals the number of fixed points under the $d$th power of the map. Of the apparent instances found in our experiment, we prove 34 new instances of the CSP and conjecture three more. Our results are organized largely by orbit structure, proving instances of the CSP for involutions with ${2}^{n-1}$ fixed points and ${2}^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor }$ fixed points, as well as maps whose orbits all have the same size. The FindStat maps which exhibit the CSP include a map constructed by Corteel (using a bijection of Foata and Zeilberger) to swap the number of nestings and crossings, the invert Laguerre heap map, a map of Alexandersson and Kebede designed to preserve right-to-left minima, conjugation by the long cycle, as well as reverse, complement, rotation, Lehmer code rotation, and toric promotion. Our results combined with prior work of the last 5 authors in (Math. Comp. 93, 921–976) show that, contrary to common expectations, actions that exhibit homomesy are not necessarily the best candidates for the CSP, and vice versa.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBishal Deb (https://www.csauthors.net/bishal-deb/)\nciteturn4search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Cyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions.\n\n# Bishal Deb\n\nOrcid: 0000-0001-5345-1751\n\nAccording to our database^{1}, Bishal Deb authored at least 8 papers between 2018 and 2026.\nCollaborative distances:\n\n  * Dijkstra number^{2} of four.\n  * Erdős number^{3} of three.\n\n## Timeline\n\n#### Legend:\n\nBook  In proceedings  Article  PhD thesis  Dataset  Other \n\n#### On csauthors.net:\n\n  * Embed bibliography on a website\n  * Download BibT_{e}X file [beta]\n\n2026\n\nCyclic sieving phenomena via combinatorics of continued fractions.\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n\nBishal Deb\n\nDiscret. Math., 2026\n\nThron-Type Continued Fractions (T-Fractions) for Some Classes of Increasing Trees.\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n\nVeronica Bitonti\n\n,\n\nBishal Deb\n\n,\n\nAlan D. Sokal\n\nElectron. J. Comb., 2026\n\n2025\n\nContinued fractions using a Laguerre digraph interpretation of the Foata-Zeilberger bijection and its variants.\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe cyclic sieving phenomenon | SymCat (https://www.symmetricfunctions.com/cyclic-sieving.htm)\nciteturn4search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Research situation: You have your favorite set \\(X\\) and a \\(q\\)-analog \\(f(q).\\) The previous theorem allows you to check (by computer, preferably) if there is some CSP phenomenon on \\(X,\\) involving \\(f(q). ... The paper gives explicit formulae for \\(q\\)-Gauß congruences and develops a universal necklace model in which beads may vary in both colour and length, producing further CSP examples from necklaces, path walks, tubings, and related objects. ... For forests they give a formula for the orbit structure, and for even cycles with no reflection edges they obtain an instance of the cyclic sieving phenomenon. ... Deb gives cyclic sieving phenomena using continued-fraction identities for permutations, set partitions, perfect matchings, and \\(D\\)-permutations [Deb25]. ...       journal = {European Journal of Combinatorics} ...     url2 = {http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X19300482}\n\n## #The cyclic sieving phenomenon\n\nSee below for the definition of the cyclic sieving phenomenon (CSP). There are many instances of the CSP, which we have put into different categories based roughly on the type of combinatorial object it concerns.\n\n  * CSP on Catalan objects.\n\n  * CSP on matchings.\n\n  * CSP on paths (including lattice paths).\n\n  * CSP on tableaux and fillings.\n\n  * CSP on words and permutations.\n\n  * CSP on other objects.\n\n### #Definition\n\nThe cyclic sieving phenomenon (CSP) was introduced by V. Reiner, D. Stanton and D. White in [RSW04]. A nice survey by B. Sagan is given in [Sag11].\n\nLet \\(X\\) be a set of combinatorial objects, \\(\\grpc_n = \\langle g \\rangle\\) be a finite cyclic group of size \\(n,\\) and \\(f(q) \\in \\setN[q].\\) Then the triple \\((X,\\grpc_n,f(q))\\) is said to exhibit the cyclic sieving phenomenon if for all \\(d \\in \\setN,\\) we have \\[|\\{x \\in X : g^d \\circ x =x \\}| = f\\left(\\exp\\left(2\\pi i \\frac{d}{n}\\right)\\right).\\] In words, \\(f(q)\\) evaluated at certain roots of unity gives the number of elements in \\(X\\) fixed by powers of \\(g.\\) Note that \\(f(1) = |X|,\\) so in many instances, \\(f(q)\\) is a \\(q\\)-analog (or \\(q\\)-enumeration) of the set \\(X.\\) The cyclic action need not be faithful. If \\(\\grpc_h\\) acts with a kernel of order \\(d,\\) then the CSP is equivalent to the faithful quotient action of \\(\\grpc_{h/d}\\) together with a polynomial \\(f'(q)\\) satisfying \\(f(q)=f'(q^d)\\); see [BR11, Sec. 2.5].\n\nGeng-Rui Zhang studies realizable and almost realizable integer sequences, where \\(a_n\\) counts periodic points of period dividing \\(n\\) for a self-map [Zha24]. This gives a useful neighboring dynamical viewpoint on root-of-unity and fixed-point phenomena: many classical combinatorial sequences are realizable, while Catalan, Motzkin, and Schröder-type sequences are not almost realizable.\n\nProposition\n\nLet \\(\\xi\\) be a primitive \\(n^\\thsup\\) root of unity, and suppose that \\(f \\in \\setN[q]\\) satisfies \\(f(\\xi^j) \\in \\setZ\\) for all \\(j \\in \\setZ.\n## #Lyndon-like cyclic sieving\n\nIn [ALP19], we consider a special type of cyclic sieving phenomenon, on a family \\(\\{X_n\\}_{n=1}^\\infty\\) of combinatorial objects. This idea captures the case where fixed points in \\(X_n\\) under the group action are in bijection with smaller members of the family.\n\nDefinition\n\nLet \\(\\{(X_n, C_n, f_n(q))\\}_{n=1}^\\infty\\) be a family of instances of CSP. The family is Lyndon-like if for all \\(n\\geq 1,\\) \\[f_{n/m}(1) = f_n\\left( e^{\\tfrac{2 \\pi i}{m}} \\right), \\text{ whenever } m|n.\\] By the definition of CSP, \\[f_n\\left( e^{\\tfrac{2 \\pi i}{m}} \\right) = |\\{ x \\in X_n : g^{n/m}(x) = x \\}|,\\] where \\(\\langle g \\rangle = C_n.\\) The family is therefore Lyndon-like if and only if for every \\(d|n\\) the number of elements in \\(X_n\\) fixed under \\(g^{d}\\) is equal to \\(|X_{d}|.\\)\n\nThe connection with counting fixed-points under iterations of a map is also discussed in the earlier work [Zar08], where many related notions are discussed.\n\nThere are several examples of Lyndon-like families of CSP. \n### #Toric promotion with refraction\n\nA. Adams, C. Defant, and J. Striker introduce toric promotion with reflections and refractions [ADS24]. This is a graph-labeling dynamical system motivated by combinatorial billiards in toric arrangements. For forests they give a formula for the orbit structure, and for even cycles with no reflection edges they obtain an instance of the cyclic sieving phenomenon.\n\n### #Permutation CSP from FindStat\n\nA. Adams, J. Elder, N. Lafrenière, E. McNicholas, J. Striker, and A. Welch give a computer-assisted analysis of cyclic sieving on permutation maps and statistics from FindStat [AELM+25]. They prove several new CSP instances and record further conjectures, including examples involving long-cycle conjugation, reverse, complement, Lehmer-code rotation, and toric promotion. B. Deb gives cyclic sieving phenomena using continued-fraction identities for permutations, set partitions, perfect matchings, and \\(D\\)-permutations [Deb25]. The proofs pass through weighted lattice paths and include involutions exchanging crossings and nestings, including a Genocchi–Corteel involution for \\(D\\)-permutations. M. Bousquet-Mélou and C. Krattenthaler prove cyclic sieving phenomena for corner-rooted plane trees and tree-rooted planar maps [BK25]. They treat several tree classes and root-moving cyclic actions, including actions restricted to leaves, non-leaf corners, or corners at nodes of prescribed degree.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nΑποτελέσματα αναζήτησης - \"CONTINUED FRACTIONS\" :: Library Catalog (https://vufind2.lib.aegean.gr/EDS/Search?dfApplied=1&filter%5B%5D=EXPAND%3A%22fulltext%22&filter%5B%5D=EXPAND%3A%22relatedsubjects%22&lookfor=%22CONTINUED+FRACTIONS%22&page=8&type=S